      [`Selling, co-ownership and exit — when selling beats holding`,
        ['ul', `**Hold if:** the net yield plus realistic price growth beats what you would earn on the after-tax sale proceeds elsewhere (compute it), the property is in a growing area, and you can stay diversified.`, `**Sell if:** the net yield is below an FD, you are over-leveraged, the flat needs heavy repairs, the family needs liquidity, or the property is a source of disputes.`, `**Do the sum:** (price − selling costs − capital-gains tax) invested at an assumed after-tax return vs (net rent + price growth). Use **Tax on Selling a Property** and **Rental Yield & Property Return**.`, `**Tax on sale (resident):** long-term after 24 months — 12.5% without indexation, or 20% with indexation for property bought before 23 July 2024 if lower; exemptions under Sections 54, 54F and 54EC if you reinvest (see the Advanced Tax guide). Buyers deduct **1% TDS** (Section 194-IA) on the consideration when it is ₹50 lakh or more (Form 26QB).`, `**Sale steps:** price and valuation → buyer search → agreement to sell with advance → checks of title and loan closure → sale deed registration and stamp duty (paid by the buyer) → possession and handover of documents → mutation and tax filings.`, `**Co-owners:** put the share, decision rights and exit rule in a written agreement; each co-owner is taxed on their share; partition or buy-out is easier when it is agreed in advance.`, `**Inherited property:** capital gains use the original owner's cost and holding period (see the Estate guide).`]],
      [`Questions landlords ask`,
        ['ul', `**“How big a deposit should I take?”** Follow the local market, but write the refund terms clearly. A larger deposit reduces risk but can reduce demand.`, `**“Can I raise the rent mid-term?”** Only if the agreement allows. Otherwise at renewal.`, `**“The tenant is not paying — what now?”** Written reminders → a formal notice → negotiation or mediation → a legal notice and, if needed, an application to the rent authority or civil court. Do not cut utilities or lock the tenant out; that can be illegal.`, `**“Can I rent to a relative?”** Yes; use the same agreement, bank transfers and tax treatment.`, `**“Is the deposit taxable?”** Not when received. If you keep part for damage or dues, that part is income.`, `**“Do I need to register for GST?”** Not for residential rent from an individual; for commercial rent above the turnover limit, yes.`, `**“Should I prepay my home loan or keep the flat on rent?”** If the loan rate exceeds the net yield plus likely growth, prepaying is a safe, guaranteed return; see the Loans guide.`]]
